morgan: Quick question — observed weird behavior in the JS client when a long streaming request hits a 429 mid-stream. Client reconnects and the server shows duplicate processing. Should the SDK automatically retry mid-stream with exponential backoff, or surface the 429 to the app so it can decide?

tara: Short answer: don't blindly retry the whole stream mid-flight. For streams we generally prefer resume tokens or explicit idempotency keys. If the server returns Retry-After, honor it; otherwise back off with jitter. :thinking_face:

cheng: FWIW we hit this with token refresh races — auth refresh causes a 401 then the client replays the stream which looked like a duplicate request. We mitigated by attaching an idempotency-key per logical operation and de-duping on the server.

sam: Re: Retry-After — Node HTTP clients sometimes give you ms vs s confusion. Server sends `Retry-After: 120` (seconds) -> make sure to convert correctly. Also respect `retry-after` in lower-case.

morgan: Good call on idempotency-key. For streaming chat we already emit partial messages to the app; re-sending partials is messy. How have others implemented resume tokens? Example flow would be nice.

tara: Example pattern we use: server yields a `resume_cursor` every N tokens. If stream dies with 429 you reopen stream with `?resume_cursor=...` and server starts from that cursor. Less duplication and weird partials.

cheng: If you can't do resume, do this on SDK side: 1) on 429 with Retry-After -> delay then reconnect 2) include same `idempotency-key` so server can detect duplicates and fast-forward 3) if server lacks idempotency support, expose a mode `fail-fast-on-429` so app can decide.

morgan: Pseudocode sketch for JS SDK retry behavior I was thinking — open to corrections:
```js
async function streamWithRetries(opts) {
  for (let attempt=0; attempt < 5; attempt++) {
    const res = await openStream(opts);
    if (res.status === 200) return res.stream;
    if (res.status === 429) {
      const ra = parseRetryAfter(res.headers['retry-after']);
      await sleep(ra ?? backoff(attempt));
      continue;
    }
    if (res.status >= 500 && res.status < 600) {
      await sleep(backoffWithJitter(attempt));
      continue;
    }
    throw new Error('unrecoverable');
  }
  throw new Error('max retries');
}
```

sam: Looks solid. Couple of notes: backoffWithJitter should use full jitter (sleep = random(0, base*2**attempt)) to avoid thundering herd. Also cap max backoff and include a circuit-breaker if many clients keep getting 5xx.

tara: Language-specific quirks: in Python async streams you can get partial chunk events and replays if you reuse the same generator. In Node, `http2` sessions can emit `aborted` and you need to tear down the session before reconnecting. SDK should hide these details and provide a simple `onResume(cursor)` hook to app.

cheng: Important: distinguish between idempotent requests (e.g., embeddings, pure generation with idempotency-key) vs non-idempotent side-effects. For any request that changes server-side state, we should not auto-retry unless the app explicitly opts in. Document this clearly in SDK docs.

devex-bot: SDK v1.8.2 released — includes retry-after parsing fix and a new `resume_cursor` helper for streams. See release notes: https://git.internal/release/1.8.2 :tada:

morgan: Awesome — I'll open a PR to add: honor Retry-After (seconds/ms handling), full-jitter backoff, idempotency-key header support, and a `failFastOn429` option for users who prefer app-level handling. Assigning to @cheng for review.

cheng: Happy to review. Also add tests for mid-stream 429 -> resume flow and token refresh race simulation.

sam: If you want, I can add a tiny Node-specific e2e to CI that simulates `http2` aborts + 429.

morgan: Please do. I'll drop a short RFC in the repo outlining heuristics. Thanks all. :raised_hands:
